{"id":"7c5b390b-ba66-45ad-b8e2-63a97dc348bd","object":"chat.completion","created":1785034560,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None and parent in children:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n\\n    # Initial retained set (all ids)\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending for ties\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        # Check if removable, not root, not required symbol\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any child is still retained\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        # Ensure all required symbols still represented after removal\\n        # (This node does not carry a required symbol, and required-symbol nodes are never removed,\\n        #  but check to be safe)\\n        symbol_ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            has_symbol = False\\n            for other in node_map.values():\\n                if other['symbol'] == sym and other['id'] in retained and other['id'] != nid:\\n                    has_symbol = True\\n                    break\\n            if not has_symbol:\\n                symbol_ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not symbol_ok:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Remove node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. Based on the fixture payloads, we have a tree of nodes with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also a required_symbols list. The task seems to be a reduction problem: given a tree, we want to remove some nodes (those with removable=True) while preserving required symbols and possibly other constraints. The output should be two lists: removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", \"interestingness oracle\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\". But we don't have a grammar or interestingness oracle. However, the fixture cases show simple trees with root (depth 0), and children at depth 1, grandchildren at depth 2. The required_symbols are root and s1. So we need to decide which removable nodes to remove.\n\nThe description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nSo algorithm: traverse nodes in order of depth descending (deepest first), then within same depth tie-break by node ID (maybe ascending? \"stable tie break at equal depth\" - typically alphabetical/numeric order). For each node that is removable and not root and not required symbol, check if removing it would cause any required symbol to be missing. Since required symbols are symbols, not node IDs, we need to see if any node with a required symbol remains. Also \"never remove a node with retained children\" - meaning if any child is retained (not removed), then parent cannot be removed? Actually careful: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is not removed (i.e., will be retained), then that node cannot be removed. This implies we process in order and when we consider removing a node, we check that all its descendants that are retained? Or it's simpler: we should only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children have all been removed? \"Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes\" suggests we try to remove leaves first, then go up. And \"never remove a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are still present (i.e., not removed). So we can only remove a node after all its children have been removed (or if it has no children). So we need to simulate removal order: for each node in depth-descending order, if removable and not root and not required symbol, and if all its children have already been removed (or it has no children), and if after removal all required symbols are still present (i.e., there exists at least one node with each required symbol that is not removed), then we remove it. Otherwise we retain it.\n\nBut we also need to consider \"grammar-valid\" and \"interestingness\" - but we have no grammar, so maybe the only constraint is the required_symbols? Also note that nodes have utility values, but not mentioned in the mechanism constraints? The description mentions \"utility\" but not used in constraints. Possibly the interestingness oracle is just checking required symbols? It says \"Preserve an interestingness oracle\" which here is likely that required_symbols must remain.\n\nCheck fixture: In case 000, nodes: root (depth 0, removable false), n1 (depth1, removable false, symbol s1), n2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0), n4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1), n5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2), n6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0). Required: root, s1. So we have two nodes with symbol s1: n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable). So n4 is removable and has no children? It's depth2, no children. So we can consider removing n4. If we remove n4, s1 still present via n1. So remove n4. Then n6 is depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0. s0 not required, so check if its children? none. So we can remove n6. Then n2 is depth1, removable true, symbol s2, children: n4? But n4 is removed, so no retained children. So can we remove n2? Check required: s1 still has n1, root present. So remove n2. Then n1 is not removable, n3 not removable, root not removable. So retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5? n5 is depth2, parent n3, removable false, so retained. So retained_ids: root, n1, n3, n5 (sorted?). removed: n4, n6, n2 (in that order). Let's check if any other nodes? That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify with the expected output? Not given. However, we can infer the algorithm.\n\nEdge cases: Node with children that are retained cannot be removed. So we must process in depth order so that children are considered before parents. And we need to track which nodes are retained/removed as we go.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Build map from id to node dict.\n2. Build parent-child relationships (list of children per node).\n3. Determine initial set of retained nodes: all nodes.\n4. Process nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id (string) ascending (stable tie break). For each node in that order:\n   - If node['removable'] is False, skip (cannot remove)\n   - If node['id'] == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols? But note: \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - it says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So check if node['symbol'] is in required_symbols => skip.\n   - Check if node has any child that is currently retained (i.e., child's id not in removed set). If any retained child, skip.\n   - Tentatively remove: check if after removal, each required symbol is still represented by at least one retained node (including nodes not yet processed). Since we are processing in depth order, deeper nodes are already decided. For each required symbol, we need to ensure there exists a node with that symbol that is not removed (either already retained or will be retained later). Since later nodes are shallower and may be retained or not, we cannot assume they will be retained. However, we can only remove if the required symbols are still present among the nodes that are currently retained *and* among the nodes that we have not yet processed (which are shallower and might be removed later? But shallower nodes with retained children cannot be removed if children are retained, so if a required symbol is only on a shallower node that might later be removed, that could be a problem. But the condition says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" I think it means that at the moment of removal, the set of nodes that are currently retained (including all not yet processed but not yet removed) still contain the required symbols. But if we later remove a node that also carries a required symbol, that could break it. So we need a more careful approach: we should only remove a node if after removal, the required symbols are still present in the set of nodes that will be retained after the entire process. But that's circular. Typical algorithms like \"removal of nodes that are not needed\" process leaves first and ensure that required symbols are preserved in the remaining set. Since we process deeper first, and we never remove a node that is a required symbol, the only way a required symbol could be lost is if a node that is the only carrier of that symbol is removed. But we are not removing required-symbol nodes. So the only required symbols are root and s1. s1 is present in n1 (not removable) and n4 (removable). Since n1 is not removable, s1 will always be present. So condition is always satisfied. But in general, if a required symbol only appears on a removable node, we would not remove that node because it's a required symbol. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically true for non-required-symbol nodes? Not exactly: if a required symbol appears on a node that is not removable, it's fine. If it only appears on removable nodes, then those nodes themselves cannot be removed because they are required symbols. So the condition is essentially: for each required symbol, there must be at least one node with that symbol that is not removed. Since we never remove nodes with required symbol, this holds. Therefore we don't need to check further; just avoid removing required-symbol nodes. But to be safe, we can still check that after removal, all required symbols are present among the remaining nodes (including those not yet processed). Since non-removed nodes will remain, and required-symbol nodes are not removed, it's fine. However, what if a node with a required symbol is also \"node with retained children\"? That would prevent removal anyway. So we can just not remove nodes with symbol in required_symbols.\n\nBut the description \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" might be more nuanced if there are multiple nodes with same symbol and we remove one, still ok.\n\nThus algorithm: \n- Build children mapping.\n- Initialize removed = empty set, retained = set of all ids.\n- For node in sorted nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending:\n   if node['removable'] and node['id'] != 'root' and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols:\n        # Check children retained: any child id not in removed? If any child's id is in retained (not removed) then skip.\n        children = children_map.get(node['id'], [])\n        retain_children = [c for c in children if c not in removed]\n        if retain_children:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols: For each sym in required_symbols, ensure there exists at least one node with that symbol whose id not in removed (including this node if it had the symbol, but it doesn't since we excluded). Since we only consider nodes with symbol not required, it's fine.\n        # Actually, we should check that after removal, each required symbol is still present. Since we are removing a node that does not have a required symbol, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is some other node (either already retained or not yet processed) that has that symbol. If the only node with a required symbol is this node? But it's not because we excluded required symbols. So it's safe. But for generality, we can do a quick check: for sym in required_symbols: if no node with symbol that is not in removed (including this node? but we will remove it) exists, then skip. Since this node doesn't have the symbol, it won't affect. So we can just skip the check or implement to be safe.\n        # Proceed to remove:\n        removed.add(node['id'])\n        retained.discard(node['id'])\n- After loop, removed_node_ids = list of removed in order of removal (we need to record order). We can maintain a list.\n- retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)) (sorted as strings? natural sort).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nCheck fixture case 000: processing order with depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Tie break by id: n4 (id 'n4'), n5('n5'), n6('n6'). n4 removable true, symbol s1? required? s1 is required, so skip because symbol in required? Actually n4 has symbol s1 which is in required_symbols. So we cannot remove n4 because it's a required symbol. But earlier I thought we could remove n4 because n1 also has s1. But the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So n4 cannot be removed because its symbol is required. That changes the outcome. Let's re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So any node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed, regardless of other nodes with same symbol. So in case 000, n4 is not removable. n5 has symbol s2 not required, removable false => skip. n6 symbol s0 not required, removable true, children none, so remove n6. Then depth1 nodes: n1 (symbol s1 required, not removable), n2 (symbol s2 not required, removable true, children: n4? n4 is still retained because not removed (since it's required). n2 has child n4 retained, so cannot remove n2 because it has retained children). n3 (symbol s0 not required, removable false). So retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. removed: only n6. So retained sorted: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root? Actually root id='root', so sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? But note root is id 'root', alphabetical order: 'n1', 'n2', 'n3', 'n4', 'n5', 'root'. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is that correct? Let's test with case 001: similar structure, n4 has symbol s1, required, so cannot remove. n6 removable, no children, remove n6. Then n2 has child n4 retained, so cannot remove. So same result. case 002 same.\n\nThus the algorithm seems to yield only possibly n6 removed in these fixtures. But the task may have more complex scenarios where multiple removals occur. We'll implement generically.\n\nImplementation details: We need to parse the case argument which is a dict with keys 'nodes', 'required_symbols', and maybe others. We'll get nodes list. Build children map by iterating nodes, for each node, if parent is not None, add node id to parent's children list. Also store node by id.\n\nProcessing order: we need to sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string comparison). Since depth is given, we use that.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We'll work with copies.\n\nReturn dict with keys removed_node_ids (list of strings in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of strings). Sorted using Python's default string sorting (which is lexicographic). That should be fine.\n\nPotential edge: nodes with depth same but id as numbers? But ids are strings like \"n4\", so alphabetical.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with implementation key containing Python source as a JSON string. The source must be a valid Python def.\n\nWrite the function.\n\nWe'll include comments for clarity but not required. Ensure no external imports.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":3725,"total_tokens":5782,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3229},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}